<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Günther I of Schwarzburg-Sondershausen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1532">1532</a>–<a href="/years/1586">1586</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Johann Günther I · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-schwarzburg">House of Schwarzburg</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Günther I was a count of Schwarzburg and the founder of its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sondershausen">Sondershausen</a> line. He ruled the northern Schwarzburg lands from Sondershausen and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnstadt">Arnstadt</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-stadtilm">Treaty of Stadtilm</a> of 1599, concluded after his death, confirmed his line's share as the separate county of Schwarzburg-Sondershausen.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-gunther-i-of-schwarzburg-sondershausen.jpg" alt="John Günther I of Schwarzburg-Sondershausen" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: gescannt und bearbeitet HieRo GlyPhe · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Johann_G%C3%BCnther.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>John Günther, also called Hans Günther or Johann Günther, was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sondershausen">Sondershausen</a> on 20 December 1532, a son of Count Günther XL of Schwarzburg, known as Günther the Rich, and his wife Elisabeth, a daughter of Count Philip of Isenburg-Büdingen-Ronneburg. He was raised a Catholic and intended for a career in the church, but after his father's death in 1552 he became a Lutheran.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He spent time at the court of Elector Maurice of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> and fought on Maurice's side at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sievershausen">Battle of Sievershausen</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-alcibiades-margrave-of-brandenburg-kulmbach">Albert Alcibiades, Margrave of Brandenburg-Kulmbach</a>. Maurice's successor, Augustus, confirmed his tariff privilege on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salt-trade">salt trade</a> at Frankenhausen. He also fought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> alongside his eldest brother, Günther XLI.</p><p>From 1552 he ruled Schwarzburg jointly with his three brothers, living at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sondershausen">Sondershausen</a>. In 1571 the brothers divided the county, and his share took the name Schwarzburg-Sondershausen after its capital. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunther-xli-of-schwarzburg-arnstadt">Günther XLI of Schwarzburg-Arnstadt</a> and William I of Schwarzburg-Frankenhausen died without male heirs, John Günther inherited Schwarzburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnstadt">Arnstadt</a>, while the last brother, Albert VII, received Schwarzburg-Frankenhausen.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 16 February 1566 he married Anna, daughter of Anthony I, Count of Oldenburg, who died in 1579. They had twelve children. Four of their sons, Günther XLII, Anton Henry, John Günther II and Christian Günther I, became counts of Schwarzburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sondershausen">Sondershausen</a>, a daughter, Catharina, was deaness of Herford Abbey, and the youngest, Dorothea, married Duke Alexander of Schleswig-Holstein-Sonderburg in 1604. His mother Elisabeth had died on 14 May 1572, the year after the partition. John Günther died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnstadt">Arnstadt</a> on 28 October 1586 and is regarded as the progenitor of the Schwarzburg-Sondershausen line.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John Günther I of Schwarzburg-Sondershausen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Treaty of Stadtilm of 1599 split the Schwarzburg lands, and Count <b>John Günther I</b>'s line received Sondershausen and Arnstadt, forming Schwarzburg-Sondershausen alongside the Principality of Schwarzburg-Rudolstadt.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-schwarzburg-sondershausen">Principality of Schwarzburg-Sondershausen</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sondershausen">Sondershausen</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnstadt">Arnstadt</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.37,10.87&amp;year=1567" aria-label="Sondershausen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4796 385 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of John Günther I of Schwarzburg-Sondershausen"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5246 655h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5246 655h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.37,10.87&amp;year=1567">What was here in 1567? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
